Reference tests of fnvnx_sha256 (accepted assumption 2: SHA-256 is correct; these check our implementation against the published vectors).

nist.c   FIPS 180-4 examples ("abc", empty, 448-bit message, 1,000,000 x 'a') and the CAVP byte-oriented vector files
         SHA256ShortMsg.rsp (65) and SHA256LongMsg.rsp (64): `nist FILE...`.
monte.c  CAVP SHA256Monte.rsp (100 checkpoints, 1000 iterations each): `monte FILE`.

Vector files: fnv-nx-build tools/docs-d3d9 commit efe9ae7f (NIST shabytetestvectors.zip, members
shabytetestvectors/SHA256ShortMsg.rsp, SHA256LongMsg.rsp, SHA256Monte.rsp; listed in that mirror's manifest).
Total: 4 + 65 + 64 + 100 = 233 vectors, 0 mismatches required.

Host build (ASan/UBSan):
  cc -O1 -fsanitize=address,undefined -Ilib/sha256/include lib/sha256/tests/nist.c lib/sha256/sha256.c -o nist
  cc -O1 -fsanitize=address,undefined -Ilib/sha256/include lib/sha256/tests/monte.c lib/sha256/sha256.c -o monte
It becomes a test root (class test, reference = the NIST files via the pinned external) when the test-root shape exists.

Vectors are committed in tests/vectors/ (public NIST CAVP data, SHAVS) with SHA256SUMS; they are byte-identical to the
fnv-nx-build tools/docs-d3d9 efe9ae7f members. The Monte test follows SHAVS 6.4 (MD0 = Seed; per checkpoint A=B=C=MD, 1000 iterations of
MD = SHA256(A||B||C), A=B, B=C, C=MD; the checkpoint MD seeds the next). The FIPS 180-4 examples are the ones of the standard (appendix B).
Where it runs: no test root exists yet (L1 test-root shape); until then on the host as above. It needs an oracle record (reference kind: spec = FIPS 180-4 / CAVP).

NOTE: lib/sha256 is guest only for now. The host and sanitizer variants wait for L1 (a target per variant, or a separate host root; the asan variant
is guest ILP32 and needs the boot machine's shadow memory), and `variants` is added to build.json when w15 is in the trunk. The host ASan/UBSan build of
the loader Makefile compiles ../lib/sha256/sha256.c by path in the meantime.
Test sources in this directory are compiled as units of the product root by the current recipe rule; they move to a test root when it exists.
